. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * To identify the causal effect of having a daughter on support towar
> ds gender equality in the
.         * household (as measured by behaviours), we use the panel structure o
> f the data and employ a
.         * difference-in-differences (DiD) approach. Specifically, we compare 
> within-individual changes
.         * in support towards gender equality before and after the birth of a 
> child between two groups:
.         * parents whose first child is a daughter (treatment group) and those
>  whose first child is a
.         * son (control group). As the gender of the first-born child is rando
> m by nature, we can
.         * exploit this exogenous variation to identify causal treatment effec
> ts. We use a Two-Way-
.         * Fixed-Effect setting accounting for individual, household, and surv
> ey year fixed effects. To
.         * measure support for gender equality (by behaviors), we use a variab
> le that states the average
.         * weekday amount of time an individual spends on all of the following
>  tasks compared to their
.         * partner: running errands, household chores, child care and care for
>  other persons.
. 
.         * CHANGED SPECIFICATION: In the PAP (as writte above) we wanted to co
> ntrol for household fixed effects. This
.         * can not be done, as the variable of interest (daughter) is constant
>  across households and would therefore
.         * be fully absorbed in the household fixed effects. For the regressio
> n model we corrected this and REMOVED
.         * the household fixed effects
.         
.         xtset pid syear

Panel variable: pid (unbalanced)
 Time variable: syear, 2005 to 2019, but with gaps
         Delta: 1 unit

.         xtreg time_allocation post_dum##daughter age age2 i.abitur labor_earn
>  i.pgemplst, fe vce(cluster hid)
